    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d49-a3">

                <pb facs="0117" n="49" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg49"/>

                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure 3—Translation]</seg>
                </opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chief of the Political Department of the German
                        General Government in Belgium</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Von der Lancken</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Minister in Belgium</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Whitlock</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">I 6940</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Brussels</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-09-12">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">September 12, 1915</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Minister</hi>: Replying to the note Your
                    Excellency addressed to me under date of the 31st last, I have the honor to
                    advise you that Miss Edith Cavell was arrested on August 5th and that she is at
                    present in the military prison of St. Gilles.</p>
                <p>She has confessed, to having hidden in her home English and French soldiers as
                    well as Belgians of the age to bear arms, all desirous of going to the front.
                    She has also confessed to having furnished these soldiers with the money
                    necessary to travel to France and to having aided their departure from Belgium
                    by procuring guides to conduct them secretly across the Dutch frontier.</p>
                <p>The defense of Miss Cavell is in the hands of the Attorney Mr. Braun who has put
                    himself into communication with the appropriate German authorities.</p>
                <pb facs="0118" n="50" xml:id="pg50"/>
                <p>Inasmuch as the General Government, for reasons of principle does not permit
                    prisoners to have intercourse with anyone, I regret exceedingly not to be able
                    to procure permission for Mr. de Laval to see Miss Cavell while she is in close
                    custody (<hi rendition="#italic">au secret</hi>).</p>
                <p>I embrace this opportunity [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lancken</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
